PLACES=[
N('eagle-bridge-hoosic-river','Eagle Bridge: the 1899 iron bridge over the Hoosic','Eagle Bridge','renss','landmark','standing',42.95144,-73.39073,'site','public','drive','','1899',
  'The hamlet in the northeast corner of the Town of Hoosick was called Kingsmills in the early 1800s, after John King\'s large grain mill. A pontoon bridge crossed the Hoosic here until 1827, when Ben Joslin\'s cattle wrecked it and a 112-foot covered bridge replaced it; Caroline Thayer painted an eagle on each end, and the bridge and then the hamlet took the name. In 1899 the Groton Bridge Company built an iron bridge, and the village asked for two cast-iron eagles, made by John King, for its ends. The hamlet grew round the railroads: the Troy & Boston built the first station in 1852, and H. P. Hood ran a creamery here from 1920 to 1977 (it burned in 1984). Grandma Moses lived at Eagle Bridge for many years.',
  'The bridge carrying NY 67 over the Hoosic River, and the Eye on History panel of 2025 on NY 67 just west of River Road, which calls the 1899 bridge the current one. Whether the cast-iron eagles are still on it was not checked; neither was the Memorial Methodist Episcopal church (cornerstone 1881), whose large stained-glass window designed by the railway postal clerk John H. Pitney shows a dove carrying a letter, a pony express rider, a stagecoach, a railway post office car and an 1870s engine: the panel tells of it, and whether the church and window survive is not checked.',
  'A bridge named for two painted eagles, and a window of the railway mail.',
  'NY 67 is a state highway; look from the road and the shoulders. No parking lot is mapped in the hamlet; park only where it is allowed. The Hoosic and the White Creek town line run under the bridge.',
  [HM(290171,'Eagle Bridge (Eye on History panel, 2025)'),W('Eagle Bridge, New York')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('potter-tavern-potter-hill','Captain John Potter\'s tavern, Potter Hill','Potter Hill','renss','hotel','standing',42.84910,-73.40814,'area','view','drive','hotels','',
  'Potter Hill on the old road between Troy and Bennington took its name from Captain John Potter, who kept a house and tavern here. On the evening of June 24, 1806, his horses shied, he fell from his wagon and a barrel of cider fell on him and killed him at 53. The road was the Old Hosick Road of 1742; three stage taverns stood about three miles apart along it, and in 1833 it was straightened and laid with field stone as the Stone Road. The hamlet also had a post office and store kept by the Agans, and Santus\' Tea House (now the Man of Kent), with a picnic and swimming ground used into the 1960s.',
  'The Eye on History panel of 2025 in the Potter Hill Barn parking lot says Potter\'s house and tavern still stand at the corner of NY 7 and Spicer Road; which building on the corner it is was not checked, so the pin is the road junction. Look from the road. The three-sided monument to Potter that the panel describes stands in a gully by a creek near the NY 7 and NY 22 junction, about 5 km east; its exact place was not found. The Potter Hill Barn stands where a barn about 270 years old burned after a lightning strike in 2002.',
  'Killed by a barrel of cider in 1806, and remembered by the hill.',
  'Private house; look from NY 7 or Spicer Road. The panel is in the parking lot of the Potter Hill Barn, a shop at 3862 NY 7, about 200 m west (that is where the parking point is; it is the shop\'s lot, so use it only as a customer or with leave).',
  [HM(290218,'Potter Hill (Eye on History panel, 2025)')],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.84885,-73.41156],thn='Potter Hill Barn lot, NY 7 (a shop\'s lot)',brick='?'),
N('west-hoosick-hamlet','West Hoosick: the church, cemetery and 1927 schoolhouse','West Hoosick','renss','town','standing',42.89953,-73.42441,'area','view','drive','','1784–',
  'A farm hamlet in the west hills of Hoosick, begun about 1784 when Silas Herrington came from Scituate, Rhode Island. It had a grist mill, a sawmill, a blacksmith, a union store financed by the families round it, and a post office; the subscription list to build the West Hoosick Church is dated April 24, 1845. The district school beside the grist mill burned in 1926 and a second was built in 1927; it is now the fire house. The panel says the west hills held more farms to the mile than any other part of Hoosick. Three miles east, at Pine Valley, Spicer and Ford Roads, the mini hamlet of South West Hoosick had an Adventist Christian church (1858, burned 1956, a stone marks its place) and District School 13 (1812–1956, now a house); Henry Dopking, believed a soldier of the British 60th Regiment in the Seven Years\' War, lies on his own land on Ford Road under a headstone that still stands (1737–1800).',
  'The Eye on History panel on Pine Valley Road (County Route 103) west of West Hoosick Road, the 1927 school that is now the fire house, the church (OpenStreetMap names a West Hoosick Baptist Church on Wilson Hill Road, 600 m south; whether it is the 1845 building was not checked) and the West Hoosick Rural Cemetery (association formed 1882). Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Public roads; look from the road. The church and fire house are private; the cemetery\'s rules were not checked. Park only where it is allowed.',
  [HM(290209,'West Hoosick (Eye on History panel, 2025)')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('hoosick-corners-tavern','Hoosick Corners: the Wilcox tavern and the first Baptist church site','Hoosick','renss','town','standing',42.86234,-73.32831,'area','view','drive','','before 1814',
  'At the corner of NY 7 (Mapletown Road) and South Street, the state markers of 1936 record an early tavern kept by David Wilcox before 1814, later the home of Dr. John Warren (1806–1882). A second state marker of 1961 at the same corner says Chester A. Arthur, president 1881–1885, lived in Hoosick. About 2 km east, at NY 7 and East Hoosick Road, a third marks the site of the first Hoosick Baptist Church, organized March 16, 1785, whose first building went up in 1792 (Elder Samuel Rogers was its first regular pastor in 1797).',
  'The markers at the two road corners. Whether the Wilcox tavern itself still stands, and which house it is, the markers do not say and was not checked: the pin is the marker corner. Nothing of the 1792 church is described.',
  '',
  'Public roads; look from the road. Private houses round the corners. Park only where it is allowed.',
  [HM(97252,'Early Tavern'),HM(97253,'Chester A. Arthur'),HM(97251,'Site of First Hoosick Baptist Church')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('old-maple-grove-cemetery-hoosick-falls','Old Maple Grove Cemetery (Walter A. Wood\'s grave)','Hoosick Falls','renss','burial','standing',42.89035,-73.34938,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1854–',
  'The Hoosick Falls Cemetery Association was incorporated in 1854 (the 1880 county history says it formed in 1851 and bought 12 acres south of the village on the west side of the road); its first president, Erastus Ball, was among the first buried, in 1855. Here lie many of the people who built the village: Walter A. Wood, maker of mowers and reapers and a congressman, buried in 1892; Charles A. Cheney, banker and the library\'s benefactor; the collector Lyman Wilder; and Harris Smith Hawthorne, a Civil War Medal of Honor sergeant. The first mausoleum, the Robertson family\'s, was built in 1932. Grandma Moses (1860–1961) is buried in Maple Grove too, by the Elks auxiliary\'s marker in the village; burial lists put her in the newer Maple Grove ground, whose place beside the old one was not checked.',
  'The Pomeroy marker at the entrance on Main Street (County Route 95), the Veterans Circle of 14 Civil War graves restored by the historical society in 2006, and the Wood and Cheney plots. The society publishes a map of Old Maple Grove (on its cemeteries page).',
  'The mower king, the banker and Grandma Moses on one hill.',
  'Cemetery of the Hoosick Falls Cemetery Association at 210 Main Street; walk in by day and keep to the drives. Its hours and rules were not checked. No lot is mapped; park on the cemetery drives only where it is allowed, or in the village.',
  [L('William G. Pomeroy Foundation: Old Maple Grove','https://wgpfoundation.org/historic-markers/old-maple-grove'),HM(124797,'Old Maple Grove'),HM(13999,'Grandma Moses'),L('Hoosick Township Historical Society: cemeteries (with a map of Old Maple Grove)','https://hoosickhistory.com/cemeteries')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('louis-miller-museum-hoosick-falls','Louis Miller Museum (Hoosick Township Historical Society)','Hoosick Falls','renss','civic','standing',42.89520,-73.35022,'exact','open','walk','','1982',
  'The Hoosick Township Historical Society, founded in 1974, opened its museum in August 1982 in a house on Main Street that Hyman and Bessie Miller had bought in 1924; the family ran Miller\'s Supermarket in the village until 1973 and gave the property in 1981 in memory of their brother Louis. The museum holds Grandma Moses memorabilia, a growing collection on the Battle of Bennington, which was fought in the town, and what the society calls one of the best collections anywhere on the Walter A. Wood company, the great maker of horse-drawn farm machines whose works made the village.',
  'The house at 166 Main Street. Inside, visitors are asked to touch the exhibits and may try on antique clothing (the society\'s page). The building\'s own age and fabric were not checked.',
  'Grandma Moses and the Wood mowers, in a grocer\'s house.',
  'Run by the Hoosick Township Historical Society. Its own page gives no opening hours that could be read; a 2014 town news item spoke of second-Saturday openings, which was not read and may be out of date. Ask the society before going. Street parking in the village.',
  [HTHS],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('cheney-library-hoosick-falls','Cheney Library','Hoosick Falls','renss','civic','standing',42.90162,-73.34902,'exact','open','walk','','1922–1923',
  'The village library was chartered in 1907. Charles Cheney, president of the People\'s National Bank, left money for a new building in 1912; its cornerstone was laid in October 1922 and it opened to the public on November 23, 1923. It is still the village\'s public library.',
  'The library building at 73 Classic Street (NY 22) at High Street, with a Pomeroy marker of 2026. What it is built of and the interior were not checked.',
  '',
  'Public library. Its own site draws its pages by script and no hours could be read; go in during library hours. Street parking on Classic Street.',
  [HM(303864,'Cheney Library'),L('Cheney Library','https://www.cheneylibrary.org/')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('first-meeting-house-site-hoosick-falls','First Meeting House site and Nathaniel Shipman\'s burial ground','Hoosick Falls','renss','burial','marker',42.89893,-73.35052,'area','view','walk','cemeteries','1800',
  'Two markers stand together on Main Street at Hall Street. One, a state marker of 1938, records the first meeting house in Hoosick Falls, put up in 1800 for the "Warren Society of Hoosick" and used by the Baptist church organized in 1847. The other, of 2019 (New York Folklore and the Pomeroy Foundation), says the burial ground here holds Nathaniel Shipman, the inspiration for James Fenimore Cooper\'s Natty Bumppo.',
  'The two markers. How much of the burial ground can be seen, and whether any stone for Shipman can be found, was not checked.',
  'The grave of the man behind Natty Bumppo.',
  'On Main Street in the village; look from the street. The ground\'s owner was not checked. Street parking.',
  [HM(125477,'First Meeting House in Hoosick Falls'),HM(147932,'Natty Bumppo')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('brimmer-farm-grave-hoosick','Brimmer Farm: George Brimmer\'s grave (1755)','Hoosick','renss','burial','marker',42.82644,-73.30990,'area','view','drive','cemeteries','1754–1755',
  'A farm settled about 1754 in the Hoosick valley, then the edge of the frontier. On June 2, 1755, it was attacked by Indians in the opening months of the French and Indian War, and George Brimmer was killed and buried here; the road it is on is still called Indian Massacre Road.',
  'The marker on Indian Massacre Road (County Route 96), about a mile south of Green\'s Crossing. Whether a grave can be seen was not checked.',
  '',
  'Roadside marker by private farmland; look from the road and do not go onto the fields. Park only where it is allowed.',
  [HM(124850,'Brimmer Farm')],
  brick='?'),
N('glass-lake-rensselaer-glassworks','Rensselaer Glassworks and Glass House Village site, Glass Lake','Sand Lake','renss','industry','marker',42.63019,-73.53146,'area','view','drive','','1804–1852',
  'Jeremiah Van Rensselaer founded the Rensselaer Glass Factory on Rouses Lake, now Glass Lake, in 1804 (some say 1788; a 2021 article prefers 1806, when the lease passed to Thomas Tillotson, Elkanah Watson, Elisha Jenkins and George Pearson). It made window glass and bottles for local merchants on a wood lease of 5,000 acres from the patroon. The village round it had 34 workers\' houses, a store, a pottery, an ashery, a school, a doctor\'s office, woodcutters\' cabins, superintendents\' houses, barns, warehouses, a cylinder packing house and boarding houses. Work ended in 1852; Glass Lake is the head of the Wynantskill, which ran the mills down to the Burden works in Troy.',
  'The Sand Lake Historical Society marker on NY 43 at Glass House Road, and the lake. Nothing of the works is described as standing; the factory cemetery was merged into the Sand Lake Union Cemetery in 1841 (already on the map).',
  'A glass town of 34 houses that vanished from a lakeshore.',
  'Roadside marker on NY 43. The lakeshore is mostly private; look from the road. No parking is mapped at the marker.',
  [SLHS,HM(65615,'Glass Lake'),L('Phil Bernnard, The Rensselaer Glassworks (Antique Bottle & Glass Collector, December 2021)','https://www.fohbc.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/RensselaerGlassworks_ABGC_Dec21.pdf')],
  brick='?'),
N('hoags-corners-anti-rent','Hoags Corners: the mill hamlet and the 1844 Anti-Rent rally','Hoags Corners','renss','town','marker',42.55643,-73.51119,'area','view','drive','','1800s',
  'Many water-powered mills worked in this hamlet during the 1800s, says the marker of the Historic Hoags Corners Association. On July 4, 1844, Dr. Smith Boughton, "Big Thunder", and his "Calico Indians" rallied tenants here against the patroons with the cry "Down with the rent!" (marker on Central Nassau Road). Boughton lived on NY 43 a few miles north from 1838 to 1888, where a marker stands at 2093 State Route 43, Averill Park, and he is buried in the Sand Lake Union Cemetery.',
  'The association\'s marker in the little park at the northeast corner of NY 66 and Dunham Hollow Road, and the Big Thunder marker 300 m away on Central Nassau Road (County Route 16). A millstone lies at the foot of the association\'s marker, and a state-style marker by the road reads "Hoag\'s Tavern, built c. 1820, famous meeting place of the Anti-Rent War 1844-45, early stop on the old Turnpike Road" (both seen in Dougtone\'s 2010 photographs on Wikimedia Commons); which building is the tavern, and whether it stands, was not checked. No mill building or ruin is described.',
  'Where the Anti-Rent War held its Fourth of July.',
  'Roadside markers on public roads; look from the road. The Town Garage Road lot is the town\'s, not a public lot; park only where it is allowed.',
  [HM(135113,'Hoags Corners'),HM(145270,'"Big Thunder"'),HM(315226,'Home of \'Big Thunder\''),L('Wikimedia Commons: Hoag\'s Tavern marker, NY 66 (Dougtone, 2010)','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_York_State_Route_66_-_4587528103.jpg')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('log-meeting-house-cemetery','Log Meeting House Cemetery (Particular Baptist Church of Schodack)','North Schodack','renss','burial','traces',42.58561,-73.59840,'site','view','drive','cemeteries','1810–',
  'The Particular Baptist Church of Schodack, known as the Log Meeting House, stood at the crossroads of North Schodack, once a village. Its record book survives from March 1810. The church is gone and only its graveyard remains, with stones of the 1810s to the 1840s; the Historical Society of Esquatak restored it in 1991.',
  'The graveyard at North Schodack Road and Sagendorf Road with the society\'s 1991 sign. The stones were not checked on the ground.',
  '',
  'Roadside graveyard; who owns it was not checked, so look from the road unless a sign says visitors are welcome. No parking is mapped.',
  [HM(148941,'Log Meeting House Cemetery'),L('Rensselaer County GenWeb: extracts from the record book of the Particular Baptist Church of Schodack','https://rensselaer.nygenweb.net/dsee.htm')],
  brick='?'),
]
